# Line endings, settled once.
#
# This tree is synced between macOS and Windows through Dropbox, and with
# nothing pinning the convention every file touched on the Windows side came
# back as a whole-file rewrite. One commit's worth: 21,000 lines of pure CRLF
# churn on top of 4,500 lines of real work, with `git blame` pointing at the
# sync rather than at whoever wrote the line.
#
# LF in the repository, whatever the checkout looks like. Git decides what is
# text by inspection, so binaries below are named only where the guess is
# worth removing.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows will not run a batch file with LF endings, so this one is checked
# out CRLF everywhere. Stored as LF like everything else.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Vendored third-party code, left byte-for-byte as it was published. It also
# carries its own .gitattributes, and normalising it would put thousands of
# lines of somebody else's source into our history for no reason.
plexora/client/external/** -text

# Never rewritten: the guess is right for all of these, but saying so keeps a
# corrupted asset off the table entirely.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.psd binary
*.ai binary
*.gz binary
*.tif binary
*.tiff binary
